---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Vemor Comps Methodology',
  description:
    'How Vemor Comps turns California PAGA settlement filings into verified comparables: programmatic source matching, document-authority hierarchy, flagged-record discipline, and minimum-sample statistics.',
};
---
<Base
  title="Methodology | How Vemor Comps Verifies PAGA Settlement Data"
  description="Every figure in a Vemor Comps report is programmatically matched to supporting text in the filed court document. Read the standards: source matching, document hierarchy, flagged-record discipline, and minimum-sample statistics."
  schema={[schema]}
  path="/methodology"
  hideWidget={true}
>
  <section class="hero" style="min-height:0; padding-bottom:24px;">
    <span class="hero-tagline">Vemor Comps</span>
    <h1>Methodology.<br /><span class="gold">Every number, checkable.</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">
      A settlement comparable is only as good as the document behind it. These are the
      standards every figure in a Vemor Comps report is held to — published so you can
      hold us to them too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-full soft">
    <div class="inner">
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:800px;">
        <h2>1. Public sources, delivered with the report</h2>
        <p>
          All matters are drawn from proposed-settlement filings on the California
          Department of Industrial Relations PAGA Case Search portal — a public
          government records source. Every report ships with the underlying filed
          documents, so any figure can be checked independently, by anyone, at any
          time. We consider a comparable that cannot be traced to its filing to be
          worthless, and we do not publish them.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Source matching, not summarization</h2>
        <p>
          Settlement economics are extracted from the filed documents, and every
          published figure is <em>programmatically matched to supporting source text</em>
          on the page it appears (matching tolerates OCR artifacts and formatting
          differences — it never tolerates a different number). A figure whose
          supporting passage cannot be located in the document is withheld or held for
          review — not estimated, not approximated, not "probably right." A missing
          value is always acceptable in a Vemor report; a plausible guess never is.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Flagged means excluded</h2>
        <p>
          Extracted records pass through automated consistency checks — internal
          reconciliation of settlement components, cross-document comparison, range
          and plausibility tests. Records that fail any check are flagged, shown in
          the report with the reason stated in plain English, and
          <strong>excluded from every benchmark statistic</strong>. A flagged record is
          never silently folded into a median. When human review resolves the issue on
          documented evidence, the decision is recorded durably — and automatically
          reopened if new conflicting evidence later appears.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Documents outrank each other — and we honor the hierarchy</h2>
        <p>
          A PAGA matter generates many filings, and they do not carry equal weight. A
          court-approved order outranks an executed settlement agreement; an executed
          agreement outranks a proposed one; operative documents outrank administrative
          notices. Where documents conflict, the higher-authority document's figures
          control and the conflict is disclosed. Where equal-authority documents
          conflict with no basis to resolve them, the record is flagged and held out of
          the statistics until the documents are read.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Units are never mixed, converted, or invented</h2>
        <p>
          Pay periods, workweeks, and workdays are different numbers. Each is reported
          only as stated in the filing; none is derived from another. Statistics are
          computed only within like cohorts — PAGA-only pay-period matters, workweek
          matters, and Class + PAGA hybrids are never blended into one median. The
          single labeled exception: a workweek rate derived from a stated workday count
          divided by five, always marked as derived.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Small samples don't get statistics</h2>
        <p>
          A median needs a minimum number of eligible records before we will print
          one; quartiles need more. Below those thresholds, the individual comparables
          still appear — the summary statistic does not. A precise-looking number
          computed over three settlements is not information; it is decoration, and we
          decline to produce it.
        </p>

        <h2>7. Pre- and post-reform are labeled, always</h2>
        <p>
          The July 2024 PAGA amendments changed the penalty regime. Every matter is
          labeled pre- or post-reform by its LWDA notice date, and era-split medians
          are reported wherever the sample supports them — because comparables from
          different statutory regimes should be weighed differently, and the report
          should say so on its face.
        </p>

        <h2>8. What this is, and isn't</h2>
        <p>
          Vemor Comps is an informational reference built from public records for
          licensed attorneys' independent judgment. It is not legal advice, and Vemor
          Consulting LLC is not a law firm. The full disclaimer accompanies every
          report.
        </p>

        <p style="margin-top:32px;">
          <a href="/comps" class="btn-primary">See Vemor Comps</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
